      "worked": "Let the common tangent be $y=mx+c$.\n\nFor $y=x^2$, equating $y$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=mx+c\\\\x^2-mx-c&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nTangency requires one intersection, so $\\Delta=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2+4c&=0\\\\c&=-\\frac{m^2}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $y=-x^2+2x-\\frac52$, equating $y$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}-x^2+2x-\\frac52&=mx+c\\\\x^2+(m-2)x+c+\\frac52&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nAgain, $\\Delta=0$. Substitute $c=-\\frac{m^2}{4}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(m-2)^2-4\\left(c+\\frac52\\right)&=0\\\\(m-2)^2-4\\left(-\\frac{m^2}{4}+\\frac52\\right)&=0\\\\2m^2-4m-6&=0\\\\(m-3)(m+1)&=0\\\\m&=3\\text{ or }-1.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $m=3$,\n$$c=-\\frac{3^2}{4}=-\\frac94.$$\nWhen $m=-1$,\n$$c=-\\frac{(-1)^2}{4}=-\\frac14.$$\nHence the common tangents are\n$$y=3x-\\frac94\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y=-x-\\frac14.$$\nBoth parameter pairs satisfy both discriminant conditions.",
